globalThis.CPRE_EXTRA_FLASHCARDS = {"source":"CPRE_Foundation_Level_Flashcards_Anki.csv","count":246,"byChapter":{"1":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1w67bfr","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"What are the three concepts that the term 'requirement' denotes (per the CPRE/IREB glossary)?","back":"1) A need perceived by a stakeholder. 2) A capability or property that a system shall have. 3) A documented representation of a need, capability, or property."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-4e0qv7","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'requirements specification'.","back":"A systematically represented collection of requirements (typically for a system) that satisfies given criteria."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1vi9cqx","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'Requirements Engineering (RE)'.","back":"The systematic and disciplined approach to the specification and management of requirements, with the goal of understanding the stakeholders' desires and needs and minimizing the risk of delivering a system that does not meet these desires and needs."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-lio7p4","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'stakeholder'.","back":"A person or organization who influences a system's requirements or who is impacted by that system. Influence can be indirect (e.g., someone following instructions from a manager)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1wczz31","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'system' (as used in the CPRE glossary / syllabus).","back":"1) In general: a principle for ordering and structuring. 2) In engineering: a coherent, delimitable set of elements that, by coordinated action, achieve some purpose. 'System' is used as an umbrella term for products, services, apps, or devices."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-otkooh","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"Name the three kinds of requirements distinguished in RE.","back":"Functional requirements, quality requirements, and constraints."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1968hqt","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"What is a functional requirement?","back":"A requirement concerning a result or behavior that shall be provided by a function of a system, including requirements for data or the interaction of the system with its environment."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-lsvvnt","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"What is a quality requirement?","back":"A requirement pertaining to a quality concern not covered by functional requirements, e.g., performance, availability, security, or reliability."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-wxb2b2","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"What is a constraint (as a kind of requirement)?","back":"A requirement that limits the solution space beyond what is necessary to meet the given functional and quality requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-u2dqnd","section":"1.1","level":"L2","front":"How can you reliably decide whether a requirement is functional, a quality requirement, or a constraint?","back":"Ask what concern it addresses: if about required results/behavior/interactions -> functional requirement; if a quality concern not covered by the functional requirements -> quality requirement; if about restricting the solution space and it is neither of the above -> constraint. (The popular 'what vs. how' rule is unreliable and often misclassifies requirements.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1ghces2","section":"1.1","level":"L2","front":"Why does the simple rule 'what the system shall do = functional, how it shall do it = quality' often fail?","back":"Because many functional requirements are specified in great detail and contain a lot of 'how' information (e.g., exact field lengths/fonts on a form), while some 'what' sounding concerns (e.g., coping with huge data volume/speed) are actually quality requirements, not functional ones."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-13tkrs5","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"What is a cyber-physical system?","back":"A system that contains both software and physical components."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-bzliph","section":"1.1","level":"L1","front":"What is a socio-technical system?","back":"A system that spans software, hardware, people, and organizational aspects."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-8t8abq","section":"1.2","level":"L2","front":"Give four ways in which adequate RE adds value when developing/evolving a system.","back":"1) Reduces the risk of developing the wrong system (early detection/correction of wrong or missing requirements is far cheaper than fixing them later). 2) Eases the intellectual complexity of understanding the problem and potential solutions. 3) Provides a basis for estimating development effort and cost. 4) Is a prerequisite for properly testing the system."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1o0vypl","section":"1.2","level":"L1","front":"List four typical symptoms/causes of inadequate RE.","back":"1) Rushing straight into building the system (schedule pressure). 2) Communication problems between involved parties (stakeholders, developers, among stakeholders). 3) Assuming requirements are self-evident (usually wrong). 4) People performing RE without adequate education and skills."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-69zeci","section":"1.3","level":"L2","front":"Where can RE be applied, and what kinds of elements do today's dominant RE application systems typically consist of?","back":"RE can be applied to requirements for any kind of system, but today's dominant case is systems where software plays a major role. Such systems typically consist of software components, physical elements (hardware, devices, sensors), and organizational elements (people, positions, processes, legal/compliance issues)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1qnl8jw","section":"1.3","level":"L1","front":"List the five forms in which requirements can occur, per their perspective.","back":"1) System requirements - what the system shall do (observed at the system/environment interface). 2) Stakeholder requirements - what stakeholders want, from their perspective. 3) User requirements - what users want, from their perspective (a subset of stakeholder requirements). 4) Domain requirements - required domain properties. 5) Business requirements - business goals, objectives, and needs of an organization."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1qi3ykg","section":"1.3","level":"L1","front":"How do user requirements relate to stakeholder requirements?","back":"User requirements are a subset of stakeholder requirements: they cover specifically the desires and needs of the users of a system."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-fgn5e8","section":"1.3","level":"L1","front":"What term do we use for requirements of a pure software system, and why are domain requirements treated separately from the ISO 29148 standard's categories?","back":"For pure software systems we speak of 'software requirements' (a form of system requirements). Domain requirements are treated as a form of their own (beyond ISO 29148) because of their importance - they are discussed further as Principle 4 (Context) in Chapter 2."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1t4foct","section":"1.4","level":"L1","front":"What are the major tasks of Requirements Engineering, and where in the syllabus/handbook is each covered?","back":"Elicitation (Ch. 4), documentation (Ch. 3), validation (Sec. 4.4), and management (Ch. 6) of requirements. Tool support (Ch. 7) can help perform these tasks."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-5ihfpb","section":"1.4","level":"L1","front":"Are requirements analysis and requirements conflict resolution separate RE tasks?","back":"No - they are considered to be part of elicitation."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-wbkuol","section":"1.4","level":"L2","front":"Why is there no single universal RE process, and what factors influence how an RE process should be tailored?","back":"There is no universal process for when/how to perform RE - a suitable RE process must be tailored from a broad range of possibilities for each system development. Influencing factors include: the overall system development process (linear/plan-driven vs. iterative/agile), the development context (relationship between supplier, customer(s), and users), and the availability/capability of stakeholders. There is also a mutual dependency between chosen work products (3.1) and the RE process (Chapter 5)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-n6u2ze","section":"1.5","level":"L1","front":"Is 'Requirements Engineer' usually an actual job title?","back":"No - in practice very few people hold the job title 'Requirements Engineer'. It is a role that people play as part of other job functions."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1ksqdbl","section":"1.5","level":"L1","front":"What three things characterize someone acting in the role of a Requirements Engineer?","back":"1) They elicit, document, validate, and/or manage requirements as part of their duties. 2) They have in-depth RE knowledge, enabling them to define RE processes, select appropriate RE practices, and apply them properly. 3) They can bridge the gap between the problem and potential solutions."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-axepq8","section":"1.5","level":"L1","front":"Give examples of job functions in which people commonly act in the role of a Requirements Engineer.","back":"Business analysts, application specialists, product owners, systems engineers, and even developers. (RE knowledge is also useful for designers, testers, system architects, and CTOs.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-1x2wul2","section":"1.6","level":"L1","front":"Besides technical/analytical skills, what 'soft skills' does a Requirements Engineer need?","back":"The ability to listen, moderate, negotiate, and mediate, as well as empathy for stakeholders and openness to the needs and ideas of others."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch1-tu1crq","section":"1.6","level":"L1","front":"Map each topic area a Requirements Engineer must learn to its chapter in the CPRE Foundation Level syllabus/handbook.","back":"Fundamental principles of RE -> Ch. 2. Documenting requirements (work products) -> Ch. 3. Elaborating requirements (elicitation & validation practices) -> Ch. 4. RE processes and working structures -> Ch. 5. Managing existing requirements -> Ch. 6. Tool support -> Ch. 7."}],"2":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1jp25tx","section":"2.1","level":"L1","front":"List all nine fundamental principles of Requirements Engineering, in order.","back":"1) Value-orientation. 2) Stakeholders. 3) Shared understanding. 4) Context. 5) Problem-Requirement-Solution. 6) Validation. 7) Evolution. 8) Innovation. 9) Systematic and disciplined work."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-114r54v","section":"2.1","level":"L1","front":"What do the nine RE principles have in common / what is their purpose?","back":"They are fundamental principles that apply to all tasks, activities, and practices in RE, and they form the basis for all the practices presented in the rest of the syllabus."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-eq8vfx","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 1 (Value-orientation): How is the 'value' of a requirement defined, and what does its 'benefit' consist of?","back":"Value = benefit minus the cost of eliciting, documenting, validating, and managing the requirement. Benefit is the degree to which the requirement contributes to: (a) building systems that satisfy stakeholders' desires/needs, and (b) reducing the risk of failure and costly rework during development."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1hs0hj3","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 1 (Value-orientation): What is the key idea behind this principle?","back":"Requirements are a means to an end, not an end in itself - they only matter insofar as they add value (benefit minus cost)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-11abf8v","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 2 (Stakeholders): Why is it not sufficient to only consider the requirements of end users or customers?","back":"Because doing so risks missing critical requirements from other stakeholders (e.g., operators, regulators, clients). Every stakeholder has a role regarding the system (user, client, customer, operator, regulator, etc.), and a stakeholder may hold more than one role at once."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-f3is3q","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 2 (Stakeholders): What is a persona, and when is it used?","back":"A persona is a fictional, archetypical description of a stakeholder, used as a substitute when a stakeholder role has too many individuals or when the individuals are unknown."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-ixd5qc","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 2 (Stakeholders): Why can conflicting requirements arise, and whose job is it to deal with them?","back":"Stakeholders may have different needs and viewpoints, which can result in conflicting requirements. It is a task of RE to identify and resolve such conflicts."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-ct3g7i","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 2 (Stakeholders): Besides end users/customers, who else should be considered stakeholders?","back":"Users who provide feedback about a system in use should also be considered stakeholders, along with other roles such as client, operator, or regulator."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-19lh4gc","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 3 (Shared understanding): What are the two forms of shared understanding, and how is each achieved?","back":"Explicit shared understanding - achieved through documented and agreed requirements. Implicit shared understanding - based on shared knowledge about needs, visions, context, etc. (not explicitly documented)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-bs587i","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 3 (Shared understanding): Name enablers and obstacles for building shared understanding.","back":"Enablers: domain knowledge, previous successful collaboration, shared culture/values, mutual trust. Obstacles: geographic distance, outsourcing, large teams with high turnover."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-9wr8rx","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 3 (Shared understanding): Name proven practices for achieving and for assessing shared understanding.","back":"Achieving it: creating glossaries, creating prototypes, using an existing system as a reference point. Assessing it: examples of expected outcomes, exploring prototypes, estimating the cost of implementing a requirement. The most important practice for reducing misunderstandings is using a process with short feedback loops."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-sr3s5o","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 4 (Context): Define 'context' and 'system boundary' as used in RE.","back":"Context: the part of a system's environment that is relevant for understanding the system and its requirements. System boundary: the boundary between a system and its surrounding context."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-125kh07","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 4 (Context): What is the 'context boundary', and how does it differ from the system boundary?","back":"The context boundary separates the RE-relevant part of a system's environment from the rest of the world, whereas the system boundary separates the system itself from its surrounding context. Both boundaries are often initially unclear and may change over time."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-pddmrd","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 4 (Context): What three context-related things must RE consider beyond the requirements inside the system boundary?","back":"1) Changes in the context that may impact system requirements. 2) Real-world requirements relevant to the system (and how to map them to system requirements). 3) Context assumptions that must hold for the system to work and meet real-world requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1g953q4","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 4 (Context): What is the 'scope' of a system?","back":"The range of things that can be shaped and designed when developing the system."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-9qssxm","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 5 (Problem-Requirement-Solution): Define 'problem' and 'solution' in relation to requirements.","back":"A problem occurs when stakeholders are not satisfied with the situation as-is. Requirements capture what stakeholders need to get rid of or simplify the problem. A socio-technical system that satisfies these requirements constitutes a solution."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-12pme3r","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 5: Do problems, requirements, and solutions always occur in that order? Give an example of an exception.","back":"No. They do not necessarily occur in this order - solution ideas may create user needs, which then must be worked out as requirements and implemented in a solution. This is typically the case when innovating."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-kz5n35","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 5: Even though problems, requirements, and solutions are intertwined, what do Requirements Engineers still aim to do, and why?","back":"They aim to separate problems, requirements, and solutions from each other as far as possible when thinking, communicating, and documenting - this separation of concerns makes RE tasks easier to handle."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1rda9vx","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 6 (Validation): What three things must be checked when validating requirements?","back":"1) Agreement about the requirements has been achieved among stakeholders. 2) The stakeholders' desires and needs are adequately covered by the requirements. 3) The context assumptions (Principle 4) are reasonable."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1y7k717","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 6 (Validation): When should validation of requirements start?","back":"Validation must start already during RE (not only after deployment), in order to control the risk of unsatisfied stakeholders from the beginning."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-ze1h8g","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 7 (Evolution): Name typical causes of requirements change.","back":"Changed business processes; competitors launching new products/services; clients changing priorities/opinions; changes in technology; changes of laws or regulations; feedback from system users requesting new/changed features. (Also: stakeholder feedback during validation and detection of faults in previously elicited requirements.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1eprgpe","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 7 (Evolution): What are the two seemingly contradictory goals Requirements Engineers must pursue regarding change?","back":"Permit requirements to change, while also keeping requirements stable."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-1sbm5bo","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 8 (Innovation): What is the core idea of this principle?","back":"Giving stakeholders exactly what they ask for misses the chance to build systems that satisfy their needs better than expected. Good RE strives not just to satisfy stakeholders, but to make them happy, excited, or feel safe - this is what innovation is about."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-op5wnm","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 8 (Innovation): On what two scales does RE shape innovative systems?","back":"Small scale - striving for exciting new features and ease of use. Large scale - striving for disruptive new ideas."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-12g03if","section":"2.2","level":"L1","front":"Principle 9 (Systematic and disciplined work): Why is a systematic, disciplined approach needed in RE even for ad-hoc developed systems?","back":"Because there is no single RE process or practice that works well in every situation; a systematic and disciplined approach to RE improves the resulting system's quality regardless of the development process used."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch2-rv1div","section":"2.2","level":"L2","front":"Principle 9: What three things does 'systematic and disciplined work' require Requirements Engineers to do (or avoid)?","back":"1) Adapt processes/practices to the given problem, context, and environment. 2) Not always use the same process and set of practices. 3) Not re-use processes/practices from past successful RE work without reflection."}],"3":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-10qmnsw","section":"3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'work product' and give the range of examples mentioned in the syllabus.","back":"A work product is a recorded intermediate or final result generated in a work process. Examples range from short-lived graphic sketches, through evolving collections of user stories, to formally released contractual requirements specification documents with hundreds of pages."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-whagxh","section":"3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"By what four characteristics are work products characterized?","back":"Purpose, representation, size, and life span."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-15z31bm","section":"3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"Give examples of work products for: (a) a single requirement, (b) a coherent set of requirements, (c) comprehensive documents/documentation structures, (d) 'other' work products.","back":"(a) Individual requirements, user stories. (b) Use cases, graphic models, task descriptions, external interface descriptions, epics. (c) System requirements specifications, product/sprint backlogs, story maps. (d) Glossaries, textual notes, graphic sketches, prototypes."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1dww82w","section":"3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"List the four representation forms of work products.","back":"Natural-language-based, template-based, model-based, and other representations such as drawings or prototypes."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-f4d5gg","section":"3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"Name the three life-span categories of work products and briefly describe each.","back":"Temporary: created to support communication and create shared understanding (discarded after use). Evolving: typically emerge over several iterations; need some metadata; change control may apply. Durable: baselined or released; need a full set of metadata; change control must be followed."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1k1kxke","section":"3.1.1","level":"L2","front":"Can a work product change its life-span category over time? Explain.","back":"Yes. A temporary work product may be converted into an evolving one (by keeping it and adding metadata); a temporary or evolving work product can become durable by being baselined or released."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-jbyags","section":"3.1.2","level":"L2","front":"What determines the appropriate abstraction level for requirements, and what key rule should be followed in small/medium work products?","back":"The choice depends on the subject being specified and the purpose of the specification. In small and medium-sized work products, requirements should be at more or less the same level of abstraction - it's important not to mix levels within such a work product."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1v8u5qj","section":"3.1.2","level":"L2","front":"In large work products like a system requirements specification, how should different abstraction levels be handled?","back":"They should be kept separate by structuring the specification document accordingly (see Section 3.6). A high-level requirement may be refined into several detailed requirements at lower, more concrete levels."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-rnzwjs","section":"3.1.2","level":"L1","front":"How do business/stakeholder requirements typically relate in time to system requirements?","back":"When expressed in durable work products (business requirements specs, stakeholder requirements specs, vision documents), they usually precede system requirements specification. In other settings, business, stakeholder, and system requirements may co-evolve."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-51vpvl","section":"3.1.3","level":"L1","front":"List the factors that influence the level of detail to which requirements should be specified.","back":"The problem/development context; the degree of shared understanding of the problem; the degree of freedom left to designers/programmers; availability of rapid stakeholder feedback during design/implementation; cost vs. value of a detailed specification; imposed standards and regulatory constraints."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-xvzj20","section":"3.1.3","level":"L2","front":"What is the fundamental trade-off involved in choosing the level of detail for requirements?","back":"Greater detail lowers the risk of eventually getting something unexpected or unspecified, but the cost of specification increases as the level of detail increases."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-anhpmw","section":"3.1.4","level":"L1","front":"List the three top-level aspects to consider when specifying requirements in work products.","back":"1) The kind of requirement (functional, quality, constraint). 2) For functional requirements, which functional aspect: structure & data, function & flow, state & behavior. 3) The context in which requirements must be understood: system context/external actors, and system boundary/external interfaces."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-pc8d6r","section":"3.1.4","level":"L2","front":"Give the syllabus example illustrating how the aspects of a work product interrelate.","back":"A request issued by a user (context) may trigger a state transition (state & behavior), which initiates an action followed by another action (function & flow) that requires data (structure & data) to provide a result to the user (context) within a given time interval (quality)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1gcu66z","section":"3.1.4","level":"L1","front":"Do all work products cover all aspects (structure/data, function/flow, state/behavior, context)? What follows when aspects are split across work products?","back":"No - some work products (notably requirements models) focus on one aspect and abstract from the others, while others (e.g. a system requirements specification) cover all aspects. When different aspects are documented in separate work products/chapters, those must be kept consistent with each other."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-192ofgs","section":"3.1.5","level":"L1","front":"List the five general documentation guidelines that apply regardless of technique used.","back":"1) Select a work product type that fits the intended purpose. 2) Avoid redundancy by referencing content instead of repeating it. 3) Ensure no inconsistencies between work products, especially across aspects. 4) Use terms consistently, as defined in the glossary. 5) Structure work products appropriately."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-zjwpz8","section":"3.1.6","level":"L1","front":"What four questions must be agreed upon when planning the work products to be used in a project?","back":"1) In which work products shall requirements be recorded, and for what purpose? 2) Which abstraction levels need to be considered? 3) Up to which level of detail must requirements be documented on each abstraction level? 4) How shall the requirements be represented in these work products?"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-9kmgxw","section":"3.1.6","level":"L1","front":"Name five advantages of defining the work products to be used early in a project.","back":"Helps plan efforts/resources; ensures appropriate notations are used; ensures results are recorded in the right work products; avoids major reshuffling/'final editing'; helps avoid redundancy (less work, easier maintainability)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-nme7zo","section":"3.2","level":"L1","front":"What are the main advantages of natural-language-based work products?","back":"Unconstrained natural language is extremely expressive and flexible; almost any requirement/aspect can be expressed in it; it's used in everyday life and taught at school, so no special training is needed to read/understand it."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-vjjw7h","section":"3.2","level":"L1","front":"What is the main disadvantage of natural-language-based work products?","back":"Natural-language texts can frequently be interpreted in different ways (ambiguity), and detecting ambiguities, omissions, and inconsistencies in such texts is difficult and expensive."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1qn3kn1","section":"3.2","level":"L2","front":"Name three ways to support writing good natural-language-based requirements.","back":"Writing short and well-structured sentences; defining and consistently using uniform terminology (via a glossary); avoiding vague or ambiguous terms and phrases (and knowing common technical-writing pitfalls)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1ev6mde","section":"3.2","level":"L2","front":"List the technical-writing pitfalls that should be AVOIDED when writing natural-language requirements.","back":"Incomplete descriptions; unspecific nouns; incomplete conditions; incomplete comparisons."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-8x9v0","section":"3.2","level":"L2","front":"List the technical-writing constructs that should be USED WITH CARE (not necessarily avoided) in natural-language requirements.","back":"Passive voice; universal quantifiers (e.g., 'all', 'never'); nominalizations (nouns derived from a verb, e.g., 'authentication')."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-4dfzmd","section":"3.3","level":"L1","front":"What are the three categories of template-based work products, and what does each provide?","back":"Phrase templates - a predefined syntactic structure for a phrase expressing a requirement or user story. Form templates - a set of predefined fields in a form to be filled (e.g., use cases, measurable quality requirements). Document templates - a predefined structure for a requirements document."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-fkfpga","section":"3.3","level":"L1","front":"List the advantages of template-based work products.","back":"Provide a clear, re-usable structure; help capture the most relevant information; make requirements/specifications look uniform; improve overall quality of requirements and specifications."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-bb2r2a","section":"3.3","level":"L1","front":"List the disadvantages/pitfalls of template-based work products.","back":"People often focus on formally completing the template rather than on content; aspects not included in the template are more likely to be omitted."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-5s5tp2","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L3","front":"Write the ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148 generic phrase template for an individual requirement, and give an example.","back":"[<Condition>] <Subject> <Action> <Objects> [<Restriction>]. Example: 'When a valid card is sensed, the system shall display the \"Enter your PIN\" message on the dialog screen within 200 ms.'"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1bqrovs","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"In requirements phrase templates, what do the auxiliary verbs 'shall', 'should', 'may', and 'will' conventionally denote?","back":"Shall = a mandatory requirement. Should = not mandatory but strongly desired. May = a suggestion. Will (or a present-tense verb without these auxiliaries) = a factual statement, NOT considered a requirement."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-142e2rb","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L1","front":"What does EARS stand for, and what is it?","back":"Easy Approach to Requirements Syntax - a set of phrase templates adapted to different requirement situations (ubiquitous, event-driven, unwanted behavior, state-driven, optional feature), designed primarily for cyber-physical systems but adaptable to others."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1btnwua","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L3","front":"Write the EARS template for a 'ubiquitous requirement' (one that must always hold).","back":"The <system name> shall <system response>."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-f064da","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L3","front":"Write the EARS template for an 'event-driven requirement'.","back":"WHEN <optional preconditions> <trigger> the <system name> shall <system response>."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1a9mhij","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L3","front":"Write the EARS template for 'unwanted behavior', and why does EARS give it its own template distinct from the event-driven one?","back":"IF <optional preconditions> <trigger>, THEN the <system name> shall <system response>. It gets a separate template because unwanted behavior (due to unexpected events such as failures, attacks, or unforeseen situations) is a major source of omissions in RE, so it deserves explicit attention."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-qu06gt","section":"3.3.1.1","level":"L3","front":"Write the EARS templates for a 'state-driven requirement' and an 'optional feature requirement'.","back":"State-driven: WHILE <in a specific state> the <system name> shall <system response>. Optional feature: WHERE <feature is included> the <system name> shall <system response>."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1kocvj","section":"3.3.1.2","level":"L3","front":"Write Cohn's classic phrase template for user stories, with an example.","back":"As a <role> I want <requirement> so that <benefit>. Example: 'As a line manager, I want to make ad hoc inquiries to the accounting system so that I can do financial planning for my department.'"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-dqlpee","section":"3.3.1.2","level":"L1","front":"Was the <benefit> part of Cohn's user story template originally optional? What is standard practice today, and why do user stories also need acceptance criteria?","back":"Cohn originally designated <benefit> as optional, but today it's standard practice to specify a benefit for every user story. Acceptance criteria accompany each user story to make it more concrete and less ambiguous, helping avoid implementation errors from misunderstandings."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-8v8jwk","section":"3.3.2","level":"L3","front":"Name the key fields of Cockburn's form template for writing use cases.","back":"Name, Precondition, Success end condition, Failed end condition, Primary actor, Other actors, Trigger, Normal flow (numbered steps), Alternate flows, Extensions, Related information."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-jv9oyv","section":"3.3.2","level":"L3","front":"Name the fields of the form template for a measurable (Planguage-style) quality requirement, and what each captures.","back":"ID (requirement number); Goal (qualitative goal statement); Scale (measurement scale); Meter (procedure for measuring); Minimum (minimum acceptable quality); OK range (acceptable value range); Desired (best-case quality)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1cr72k2","section":"3.3.3","level":"L1","front":"What is a document template, and name two sources where RE document templates can be found.","back":"A template providing a predefined skeleton structure for a document (e.g., a system requirements specification). Sources: standards such as ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148, and the popular Volere template by Robertson and Robertson."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-rkyanp","section":"3.4.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'model' and 'original' as used in RE modeling.","back":"A model is an abstract representation of an existing part of reality, or a part of reality to be created. The modeled part of reality (with respect to a given model) is called the original."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1scpqh3","section":"3.4.1","level":"L2","front":"What advantages do requirements models have compared to requirements in natural language?","back":"Relationships/interconnections between requirements are easier to understand graphically; focusing on a single aspect reduces cognitive load; modeling languages have a restricted syntax which reduces possible ambiguities and omissions."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1r5k4uv","section":"3.4.1","level":"L2","front":"What are the limitations of requirements models?","back":"Keeping models that focus on different aspects consistent with each other is challenging; information from different models needs to be integrated for causal understanding; models focus primarily on functional requirements (most quality requirements/constraints can't be modeled with reasonable effort); restricted syntax means not everything relevant can be expressed -> models and NL requirements are frequently combined."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-qpvn6n","section":"3.4.1","level":"L1","front":"List the four uses of models in RE mentioned in the syllabus.","back":"1) Specifying (primarily functional) requirements, partly or fully replacing textual requirements. 2) Decomposing a complex reality into well-defined complementing aspects, each represented by a specific model. 3) Paraphrasing textual requirements to improve comprehensibility, especially of relationships. 4) Validating textual requirements to uncover omissions, ambiguities, and inconsistencies."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-8omar5","section":"3.4.2","level":"L1","front":"What do context models specify, and name two ways to graphically express them.","back":"Context models specify a system and the actors in the system context that interact with it, including a sketch of the interfaces (what information is exchanged). They can be expressed with context diagrams (e.g., data flow diagrams / structured analysis, or tailored box-and-line diagrams) or with UML use case diagrams."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1wqsxty","section":"3.4.2.1","level":"L2","front":"In a data-flow-diagram (DFD) context diagram, what are 'terminators', and what is the difference between a 'source' and a 'sink'?","back":"Terminators are the rectangles around the central system process representing external entities. A source is a terminator that provides information/services TO the system; a sink is a terminator that takes information/services FROM the system. A terminator can act as either, depending on the data provided or retrieved."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-rj7pb4","section":"3.4.2.2","level":"L1","front":"In a UML use case diagram, what do 'actors', the 'association', and the 'system boundary' represent?","back":"Actors are the direct users or other systems interacting with the system under consideration. The association connects an actor to a use case, expressing only that the actor receives the benefit from the use case (no direction/data flow, unlike DFDs). The system boundary (a rectangle around the use cases) visually separates the system's functionality from the actors in the context."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-uwsixo","section":"3.4.2.2","level":"L2","front":"How does a UML use case diagram differ from a DFD context diagram in how it represents the system?","back":"A use case diagram models the system's functions (use cases) from a functional/user perspective, describing scope via individual functions. A DFD context diagram instead represents the whole system as one big 'black box' process."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1a2gy69","section":"3.4.3","level":"L1","front":"What do models of 'structure and data' specify, and name the common model types for this.","back":"They specify requirements for the static structural properties of a system or domain (the business objects and their relationships). Common models: Entity Relationship Diagrams (ERD), UML class diagrams, and SysML Block Definition Diagrams."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-2fj72q","section":"3.4.3.1","level":"L3","front":"In a UML class diagram, what do a class, an attribute, an association, and 'multiplicity' represent?","back":"A class represents a tangible or intangible entity (e.g., Customer, Order, Book) the system must know about. An attribute is a property maintained for a class (e.g., Book.inStock). An association is a relationship between two classes (e.g., Customer-Order). Multiplicity specifies how many instances of one class relate to instances of another (e.g., 1 customer places 1-or-more orders)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1fsnvqq","section":"3.4.3.1","level":"L2","front":"Why can't the rule 'if stock is insufficient, send a print job to the printer' be shown in a UML class diagram?","back":"Because a class diagram only models static structure (classes, attributes, associations) - it cannot express system functionality/behavior. Such a rule must be documented elsewhere, e.g. as a textual requirement or modeled with another diagram such as a UML activity diagram."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1jtfud1","section":"3.4.4","level":"L1","front":"What do models of 'function and flow' specify, and name common model types used for it.","back":"They specify the sequence of actions needed to produce results from inputs, or to execute a (business) process - including control/data flow between actions and who is responsible for which action. Common models: UML use case diagrams, UML activity diagrams, data flow diagrams, domain story models, and BPMN."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-tvayje","section":"3.4.4.1","level":"L1","front":"What can a UML activity diagram express, according to the syllabus/handbook (at Foundation Level)?","back":"The control flow of activities/actions of a (sub)system, including decisions and parallel flows, and who is responsible for which action. (Advanced elements for modeling data flow are not covered at Foundation Level.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1xgojfa","section":"3.4.4.1","level":"L2","front":"In the book-ordering activity-diagram example, what is an 'exceptional flow' versus an 'alternative flow'?","back":"The exceptional flow handles a functional faulty condition (e.g., invalid Order or Customer information triggers a notification and cancels the process). The alternative flow handles a valid-but-different path through the process (e.g., insufficient stock triggers a print job request and a redelivery notification, instead of directly shipping)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-7875hz","section":"3.4.4","level":"L1","front":"What are process models used for, and which notations can express them? Which one does the CPRE Foundation Level restrict itself to?","back":"Process models describe business or technical processes. They can be expressed with UML activity diagrams or specific process modeling languages such as BPMN. At the CPRE Foundation Level, only UML activity diagrams are used for process modeling."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-13y5bdc","section":"3.4.5","level":"L1","front":"What do models of 'state and behavior' specify, and what is the difference between a state machine and a statechart?","back":"They specify requirements for a system's or component's behavior: state-dependent reactions to events, or dynamics of component interaction. A state machine models events that trigger transitions between states and the actions performed on transition. A statechart is a state machine whose states are decomposed hierarchically and/or orthogonally."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1v9y6oo","section":"3.4.5.1","level":"L2","front":"In the book-ordering UML state-diagram example, what does the state diagram show that the class diagram cannot?","back":"The class diagram only lists the possible values of an attribute like 'status' (e.g., Validated, Paid, Delivered, Canceled) but not their order. The state diagram visualizes the actual sequence/transitions between states (e.g., that an order cannot go directly to Delivered without first being Paid), i.e., it models behavior over time, not just static structure."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-7tzfu6","section":"3.4.5.1","level":"L2","front":"In a UML state diagram, what is a 'trigger', and can a transition lead back to the same state? Give the book-ordering example.","back":"A trigger is the event from the system or its environment that causes a state transition. Yes - a transition can loop back to the same state: in the example, if an order is not yet completely picked, it stays in the 'Picked' state (self-transition) until picking is complete, only then transitioning to 'Sent'."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-220sn2","section":"3.4.6","level":"L1","front":"What do goal models represent, and what elements can they include?","back":"A set of goals, sub-goals, and the relationships between them. They may also include tasks and resources needed to achieve a goal, actors who want to achieve a goal, and obstacles that impede goal achievement."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1sqopx8","section":"3.4.6","level":"L1","front":"What are SysML block definition diagrams used for in RE, and how can they express context diagrams?","back":"They can model the structure of a system in terms of its conceptual entities and the relationships between them. They can be adapted to express context diagrams by using stereotyped blocks for the system and the actors."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1y7700k","section":"3.4.6","level":"L1","front":"What are domain story models, and what are they used for?","back":"Models that specify visual stories about how actors interact with devices, artifacts, and other items in a domain, typically using domain-specific symbols. They model function and flow and help understand the application domain in which a system will operate."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-gutwjk","section":"3.4.6","level":"L1","front":"What are interaction models, and which UML diagram type is popular for specifying them?","back":"Interaction models model the dynamic interactions between objects or actors. UML sequence diagrams are a popular means for specifying interactions between objects."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1wn3mvk","section":"3.5","level":"L1","front":"Define 'glossary' and list what it is a collection of.","back":"A glossary is a central collection of definitions of: context-specific terms, everyday terms with special meaning in the given context, abbreviations, and acronyms. Synonyms should be marked as such; homonyms should be avoided or marked as such."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1gijmc2","section":"3.5","level":"L1","front":"List the rules that apply for managing a glossary.","back":"Manage it centrally; maintain it over the entire system development; assign a responsible person/small group; use a uniform style/structure; involve stakeholders and seek agreement on terminology; make it accessible to everybody involved; make its use mandatory; check work products for proper glossary usage."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1xhmkjm","section":"3.6","level":"L1","front":"List five frequently used requirements specification documents, and three frequently used alternative documentation structures.","back":"Documents: Stakeholder Requirements Specification, User Requirements Specification, System Requirements Specification, Business Requirements Specification, Vision Document. Alternative structures: Product backlog, Sprint backlog, Story map."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1ffe6oy","section":"3.6","level":"L1","front":"How does a User Requirements Specification relate to a Stakeholder Requirements Specification?","back":"A User Requirements Specification is a subset of a Stakeholder Requirements Specification, covering only the requirements of users."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-j0fmwx","section":"3.6","level":"L2","front":"What factors influence the choice of a documentation structure and its internal organization?","back":"The chosen development process (Ch. 5); the development type and domain; the contract (a customer may prescribe a given structure); and the size of the document."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-jlr82w","section":"3.7","level":"L1","front":"What is the main purpose of using prototypes in RE, and when are they especially useful?","back":"Prototypes specify requirements by example and are used for validating requirements. They're especially useful when stakeholders don't want to write/review natural-language, template-based, or model-based work products."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-ni785i","section":"3.7","level":"L1","front":"Name the three levels of fidelity for exploratory prototypes, and briefly describe each.","back":"Wireframes - low-fidelity, built with simple materials/sketching tools, for discussing/validating design ideas and UI concepts. Mock-ups - medium-fidelity, use real screens/click-flows but no real functionality, for specifying/validating user interfaces. Native prototypes - high-fidelity, implement critical parts of the system so stakeholders can see if it will actually work/behave as expected."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1xm9t09","section":"3.7","level":"L1","front":"What distinguishes an 'evolutionary prototype' from an 'exploratory prototype'?","back":"Exploratory prototypes are used to create shared understanding/clarify/validate requirements and are discarded after use. Evolutionary prototypes are pilot systems forming the core of the system to be built - the final system evolves by incrementally extending/improving this pilot over several iterations (it is NOT discarded)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-1jir2wx","section":"3.8","level":"L1","front":"Which two quality criteria for a single requirement are considered the most important, and why?","back":"Adequacy and understandability - without them, a requirement is useless or even harmful, regardless of whether all other criteria are fulfilled."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-ev1d8n","section":"3.8","level":"L1","front":"List the six quality criteria for a single requirement.","back":"Adequate (describes true and agreed stakeholder needs); Necessary; Unambiguous; Complete (self-contained); Understandable; Verifiable."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-19023xz","section":"3.8","level":"L1","front":"List the six quality criteria for a work product covering multiple requirements.","back":"Consistent; Non-redundant; Complete (no known/relevant requirements missed); Modifiable; Traceable; Conformant."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch3-v43bml","section":"3.8","level":"L2","front":"How should the degree of fulfilment of quality criteria relate to a requirement's value, under a value-oriented RE approach?","back":"It should correspond to the value created by the requirement: requirements don't have to adhere to all quality criteria completely, but the higher a requirement's value, the more relevant the quality criteria become, in order to reduce the risk of failure."}],"4":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-13mt1kq","section":"4.1","level":"L1","front":"What three types of requirements sources are distinguished in RE?","back":"Stakeholders, Documents, and Systems (other systems)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-dg8jou","section":"4.1","level":"L2","front":"Why is identifying requirements sources described as 'an iterative and recursive process that requires constant reconsideration', and what must be understood first before sources can be identified?","back":"Because you keep discovering new sources as you learn more, so identification must be repeated and revisited throughout the project. A shared understanding of the system's context (the area between the system boundary and the context boundary) is a prerequisite for identifying the relevant requirements sources."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1rpfdpl","section":"4.1","level":"L1","front":"List the typical stakeholder roles named in the syllabus.","back":"Users (end-users), Sponsors, Managers, Developers, Authorities, Customers. People/organizations impacted by the system should also be considered (indirect) stakeholders."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1y24vtf","section":"4.1","level":"L1","front":"List the information that should (at minimum) be recorded in a stakeholder list.","back":"Name; Function (role); Additional personal/contact data; Temporal and spatial availability during the project; Relevance; Area and extent of expertise; Goals and interests in terms of the project."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-hwz386","section":"4.1","level":"L1","front":"List potential sources for identifying relevant stakeholders and stakeholder roles.","back":"Checklists of typical stakeholder groups/roles; organizational structures; business process documentation; market reports; initial stakeholders (used to identify additional stakeholders, i.e. 'snowballing')."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-b0wjbf","section":"4.1.1","level":"L1","front":"What is the difference between internal and external users, and what is a 'misuser'?","back":"Internal users belong to the organization for which the system is developed (staff, management, subcontractors) - few in number, known individually, reachable via formal channels. External users are outside the company (customers, partners, civilians) - potentially very numerous, often unknown individually, reached via marketing/incentives/panels. A misuser is someone who intentionally tries to interact with the system in a harmful way (e.g., a hacker or competitor)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-b1ogao","section":"4.1.1.2","level":"L1","front":"What is a persona, and what are the two major approaches for creating one?","back":"A persona is a fictitious individual describing a typical user group with similar needs, goals, behaviors, or attitudes. Approaches: (1) Data-driven - built from interviews, focus groups, ethnographic data (qualitative personas) or statistical analysis of a large user sample (quantitative personas). (2) Imagination - developed via brainstorming (ad hoc / proto-personas), which must later be checked and confirmed."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-pk9p2l","section":"4.1.2","level":"L1","front":"Besides being a direct source of requirements, what is another valuable use of documents as a requirements source?","back":"Documents can lead you to other sources - e.g., an internal process description may mention roles involved in that process, pointing you to new potential stakeholders."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1m52owu","section":"4.1.2","level":"L1","front":"Why must documents used as requirements sources be managed (e.g., via a document list), and what key metadata should be tracked?","back":"To guard against working with outdated or invalid information. Track date, version number, and status (draft vs. final); keep old versions archived (useful for understanding how requirements evolved); this also supports backward traceability."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-l5ndoa","section":"4.1.3","level":"L1","front":"What is the difference between 'similar systems' and 'interfacing systems' as requirements sources?","back":"Similar systems share functionality with the system to be developed (predecessor/legacy systems, competitor systems, comparable systems elsewhere) - good for detailed requirements, constraints, and delighters. Interfacing systems have a direct relationship with the system being developed, exchanging data with it as a source/sink through an interface - understanding them in detail (context, functionality, behavior) is essential."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1swyqro","section":"4.1","level":"L1","front":"List five categories of 'additional sources' for requirements beyond stakeholders.","back":"Existing and legacy systems; process documents; legal or regulatory documents; company-specific regulations; (marketing) information about potential future users. (Also: looking at similar situations in completely different domains.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-i4ogvb","section":"4.2","level":"L2","front":"What is the main task of a Requirements Engineer during elicitation, and what is a 'major point' to achieve during it?","back":"To understand stakeholders' desires and needs while ensuring requirements from all relevant sources are collected, using appropriate techniques. A major point is turning implicit demands, wishes, and expectations into explicit requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1hw08s2","section":"4.2.1","level":"L1","front":"Name the three Kano model categories (with their synonyms) used to classify requirements/features.","back":"Delighters (excitement factors, unconscious requirements); Satisfiers (performance factors, conscious requirements); Dissatisfiers (basic factors, subconscious requirements)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-u9wxvs","section":"4.2.1","level":"L2","front":"Explain a delighter using the Kano model, including why customers don't ask for it and what happens if it's absent vs. present.","back":"A delighter is a feature customers are unaware is possible, so they don't ask for it (unconscious). If absent, no one complains; if present, it can be a differentiating feature that attracts many customers (e.g., a smartphone that becomes a beamer)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-nz7rxo","section":"4.2.1","level":"L2","front":"Explain a dissatisfier using the Kano model, including why customers don't ask for it and the consequence of leaving it out.","back":"A dissatisfier is so obvious that customers can't imagine the system without it (subconscious must-have, e.g. GPS in a smartphone). If included, customers won't even notice; if left out, customers will be very upset and may refuse to use the system."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1duu696","section":"4.2.1","level":"L1","front":"How are satisfiers typically elicited, and why does adding more of them involve a trade-off?","back":"Satisfiers are explicitly asked for by customers (conscious requirements), so they are relatively easy to elicit directly. However, adding more satisfiers usually increases cost, so a cost/benefit analysis is needed to decide how many to include."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-xp41de","section":"4.2.1","level":"L2","front":"How do Kano categories tend to shift over time? Give the example from the handbook.","back":"A feature often starts as a delighter, becomes a satisfier as more competitors adopt it and customers start explicitly wanting it, and eventually becomes a dissatisfier once it's taken for granted. Example: the cell-phone camera moved from delighter -> satisfier -> dissatisfier in under 20 years."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1gelra3","section":"4.2.1","level":"L1","front":"Besides delighters, satisfiers, and dissatisfiers, what two additional Kano categories exist, and why are they useful?","back":"Indifferent ('I don't care') and reject ('I hate') requirements. They help decide whether to implement a developer-proposed feature: if customers are indifferent, it's safe to include; if it's a reject requirement, it should be avoided as a costly mistake."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-t4om74","section":"4.2","level":"L1","front":"What are the two broad categories of elicitation techniques distinguished in the syllabus, and which Kano categories does each primarily help uncover?","back":"Gathering techniques - help elicit satisfiers and dissatisfiers by investigating sources. Design and idea-generating techniques - stimulate creativity, often yielding new/innovative requirements that are often delighters."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1cfn844","section":"4.2.2","level":"L1","front":"Name the four categories of gathering techniques.","back":"Questioning techniques, Collaboration techniques, Observation techniques, Artifact-based techniques."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-8yuvkm","section":"4.2.2","level":"L2","front":"Compare interviews and questionnaires as questioning techniques: strengths and weaknesses of each.","back":"Interviews: flexible, no special tools, can elicit both high-level and detailed requirements, but time-consuming (less suited for reaching large numbers of stakeholders); usually yield satisfiers. Questionnaires: reach large groups; quantitative (closed-ended) questionnaires confirm hypotheses and are quick to evaluate; qualitative (open-ended) ones can find new requirements but are more time-consuming to prepare/evaluate; designing a good questionnaire takes significant effort."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-4es2ke","section":"4.2.2","level":"L1","front":"What is a 'requirements workshop', and what technique is often used afterward if more detail is needed?","back":"A structured meeting where a carefully selected group of stakeholders/experts work together to define, create, refine, and reach closure on deliverables representing user requirements. If more detail is needed afterward, follow-up interviews or questionnaires are appropriate."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1s9lxee","section":"4.2.2","level":"L1","front":"What is crowd-based (platform-based) Requirements Engineering, and what does it require to work well?","back":"An approach that turns elicitation into a participatory effort involving a crowd of stakeholders (especially users), leveraging the diversity of talents/expertise in the crowd. It requires an automated platform to process the large amount of resulting data and to support collaboration, knowledge sharing, and engagement."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-17ak5b0","section":"4.2.2","level":"L2","front":"Why are observation techniques particularly useful for finding dissatisfiers?","back":"Because stakeholders are observed in their normal processes without interference, revealing activities/data so common to them that they wouldn't think to mention them in interviews or questionnaires - these 'invisible' aspects are exactly where dissatisfiers hide."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-z799q3","section":"4.2.2","level":"L1","front":"Distinguish 'field observation' from 'apprenticing' as observation techniques.","back":"Field observation: the Requirements Engineer watches (mostly) end users performing activities without interfering, sometimes even without informing them (e.g., video recording for later analysis). Apprenticing: participatory - the Requirements Engineer acts as an apprentice/intern, with experienced users as 'masters' teaching them how things work, participating but not interfering, to build deep domain understanding before elicitation starts."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-17gu01c","section":"4.2.2","level":"L1","front":"Name three examples of artifact-based elicitation techniques and briefly describe each.","back":"System archaeology - extracting requirements from existing/legacy/competitor systems by analyzing documentation or implementation. Feedback analysis - mining structured or unstructured feedback (ratings, reviews, support comments) from existing systems/prototypes. Reuse of requirements - applying requirements already elicited for previous systems (saves time, but must still be validated for the new context)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1huup63","section":"4.2.3","level":"L1","front":"Name the two subcategories of design and idea-generating techniques, plus the related emerging field mentioned.","back":"Creativity techniques and Design techniques; plus the broader emerging field of Design thinking."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-geymip","section":"4.2.3","level":"L1","front":"List the preconditions identified as important for creativity to emerge.","back":"Chance (and therefore time) for an idea to come up; knowledge of the subject matter; motivation (the brain is creative only if there's a direct benefit); safety and security (useless ideas must not have negative consequences)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-t5vwyc","section":"4.2.3","level":"L1","front":"What are the key ground rules of brainstorming?","back":"Quantity prevails over quality; free association and visionary thinking are desired; building on/combining others' ideas is allowed and desired; criticizing others' ideas is forbidden, however absurd they seem. (Idea-finding is deferred from idea-analysis - judgment is postponed.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-faraqc","section":"4.2.3","level":"L2","front":"Describe the two steps of the analogy technique for generating ideas.","back":"1) Elaborate the aspects of a selected analogy in detail without referring to the original problem. 2) Transfer all identified aspects of the analogy back to the original problem. The analogy can be close (same problem in another business) or distant (e.g., comparing an organization to a living organism)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-196fhj","section":"4.2.3","level":"L1","front":"Name two popular design techniques used to explore/elaborate ideas, and what do design techniques generally rely on?","back":"Prototyping, and Scenarios & storyboards. Design techniques heavily rely on visual or tangible artifacts, team cooperation, and customer feedback."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-jxi6r8","section":"4.2.3","level":"L1","front":"What is a storyboard, and how does it relate to a scenario?","back":"A storyboard is the visual form of a scenario, typically a comic-strip-like series of panels showing the interaction of personas with the system. A scenario describes a flow of actions for a system (including the actors/users involved) and can be documented in written or visual (storyboard) form."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-szpvup","section":"4.2.3","level":"L2","front":"What are the two basic principles found in all variants of design thinking?","back":"Empathy - finding the real problem behind the given problem by understanding what stakeholders think/feel/do (human-centered design; via personas, empathy mapping, customer co-creation). Creativity - the alternation of divergent thinking (exploring widely, generating many ideas) and convergent thinking (focusing, selecting, combining into a final delivery) - the 'double diamond' pattern."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-18ap0et","section":"4.2","level":"L2","front":"Why should quality requirements and constraints get special attention during elicitation, and what tool can help elicit quality requirements?","back":"Because in practice, quality requirements and constraints often get less attention than functional requirements. A quality model such as ISO 25010 can be used as a checklist to elicit and even quantify quality requirements. Constraints can be found by considering restrictions on the solution space (technical, legal, organizational, cultural, environmental)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-91z8qi","section":"4.2","level":"L2","front":"What factors influence the choice of elicitation technique, and what generally gives the best results?","back":"Type of system; software development life cycle model; people involved; organizational setup. The best results are usually achieved with a combination of different elicitation techniques."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-b2a91z","section":"4.3","level":"L1","front":"List the four tasks involved in identifying and resolving requirements conflicts.","back":"Conflict identification; conflict analysis; conflict resolution; documentation of conflict resolution (the decisions made)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1dk27rn","section":"4.3.2","level":"L1","front":"Name the six types of requirements conflict.","back":"Subject matter conflict; data conflict; interest conflict; value conflict; relationship conflict; structural conflict."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-153m07l","section":"4.3.2","level":"L2","front":"Describe a 'subject matter conflict' and a 'data conflict', and how they differ.","back":"Subject matter conflict: the parties genuinely have different factual needs (e.g., the system must satisfy different countries' legislation) - the underlying facts can't be changed, so first document and agree on the exact nature of the conflict. Data conflict: parties refer to inconsistent data or interpret the same data differently (often due to poor communication or differing assumptions, e.g., sales estimates) - hard to detect because each side assumes their own data/interpretation is correct; repeated communication is key."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1tkdjcz","section":"4.3.2","level":"L2","front":"Describe an 'interest conflict' and a 'value conflict', and how they differ.","back":"Interest conflict: based on differing positions driven by personal/group/role-related goals; stakeholders may hide true motives behind seemingly factual arguments - resolve by identifying/strengthening shared interests. Value conflict: based on differences in stakeholders' values/principles - more individual, long-term, and stable than interests; parties insist on arguments reflecting their inner values - look for higher shared values; notoriously difficult to resolve fully."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1qu4wnw","section":"4.3.2","level":"L2","front":"Describe a 'relationship conflict' and a 'structural conflict'. Why are these two often outside the Requirements Engineer's ability to resolve directly?","back":"Relationship conflict: rooted in negative past experiences, with emotions/miscommunication overriding facts - escalation or exchanging people is often needed. Structural conflict: involves inequality of power, competition over limited resources, or structural dependencies - escalation to higher authority is often necessary. Both are often not directly about requirements, so the Requirements Engineer may not be the right party to resolve them, though they should ensure someone does."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-stu8g6","section":"4.3.3","level":"L2","front":"Compare 'agreement' and 'compromise' as conflict resolution techniques.","back":"Agreement: stakeholders discuss until they fully understand each other and agree on an option all actually prefer - time-consuming but durable if reached; common for data conflicts. Compromise: stakeholders accept an option that isn't their first preference but that they can live with (may include new elements introduced by the RE) - also durable; often used next if agreement fails, suits subject matter, interest, and structural conflicts."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1szpmzi","section":"4.3.3","level":"L2","front":"When is 'voting' an appropriate conflict resolution technique, and what precautions help it work well?","back":"Best for a relatively simple choice among a clear set of conflicting requirements. Precautions: all participants should understand the alternatives/consequences; voting should be anonymous with a neutral moderator; the voting procedure should be agreed in advance. Works for most conflict types, especially subject matter and interest conflicts; the losing party needs attention."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-bmtzj","section":"4.3.3","level":"L2","front":"What is 'overruling' as a conflict resolution technique, and for which conflict types does it work well?","back":"Transferring the decision to someone with higher authority/power than all conflicting parties (or outsourcing to a third party/expert), often used under time pressure when agreement/compromise/voting fail. Good for resolving interest and structural conflicts; the decision maker must fully understand alternatives and consequences."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1hpunkm","section":"4.3.3","level":"L2","front":"What is 'definition of variants' as a conflict resolution technique, and what is its main drawback?","back":"Building separate solutions for all conflicting requirements, usually via a configurable system with parameters for each variant. Drawback: takes a lot of time to define, and introduces growing complexity and cost in development, operation, and maintenance. Used for subject matter, interest, and value conflicts when time/budget allow."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-f6m30u","section":"4.3.3","level":"L1","front":"Name the three auxiliary conflict-resolution techniques, and what they have in common.","back":"Consider-All-Facts (CAF), Plus-Minus-Interesting (PMI), and Decision matrix. They are not usually used alone but assist the main resolution techniques by creating more insight/clarity into the alternatives; all are essentially variants or build on a decision-matrix approach."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1jif6d1","section":"4.3.3","level":"L2","front":"Explain how Plus-Minus-Interesting (PMI) works as a conflict-resolution/brainstorming tool.","back":"Participants first list the positive aspects (plus) of each alternative, then the negatives (minus), and finally interesting points needing further investigation. The alternative with the most pluses and fewest minuses is preferred."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-3hlcn7","section":"4.4","level":"L2","front":"Why must requirements be validated, and what does 'validating requirements' actually mean in practice?","back":"Validating requirements is essential for a successful system (Principle 6) - ensuring quality up front reduces wasted downstream effort. It means checking both the work products as a whole and the individual requirements within them for quality (per the quality criteria in Section 3.8)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-10grb13","section":"4.4.1","level":"L1","front":"List the four important aspects to consider in requirements validation.","back":"Involvement of the right stakeholders; separating the identification and the correction of defects; validation from different views; repeated validation."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1sy9o9a","section":"4.4.1","level":"L2","front":"Regarding 'involvement of the right stakeholders' in validation, what is the trade-off between low and high independence of validators from the elicitation team?","back":"Low independence (inviting people who already did the elicitation) is cheap/easy but risks overlooking defects due to their own focus, blind spots, or flawed assumptions. Higher independence (external reviewers/auditors) costs more time/effort but is more effective at finding severe defects, especially for higher-risk projects."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-twi4ew","section":"4.4.1","level":"L2","front":"Why should the identification and correction of defects be kept separate during validation?","back":"Because defects can influence each other (a later-found defect might invalidate an earlier 'fix'; a requirement first flagged as defective might later prove correct). Reviewers should focus on finding defects, not on fixing them; decide whether/how to fix only after the whole set of requirements has been checked."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-11jmvea","section":"4.4.2","level":"L1","front":"Name the three categories of requirements validation techniques, and which are 'static' vs. 'dynamic'.","back":"Review techniques and Sample development are static (analyze specifications without executing the system). Exploratory techniques are dynamic (focus on actual/simulated system behavior in operation)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-4l3k4r","section":"4.4.2","level":"L2","front":"Compare 'walkthroughs' and 'inspections' as formal review techniques.","back":"Walkthrough: the AUTHOR explains the work product step by step to an audience, who comment/ask questions interactively; good for early feasibility discussions or handing off a work product. Inspection: a more formal technique led by an independent moderator (not the author); inspectors check the work product against standards/objectives, usually after individual preparation with checklists; produces a detailed audit trail; often used to decide on releasing a work product, especially in (safety-)critical systems."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-162zi08","section":"4.4.2","level":"L1","front":"What distinguishes an 'informal review' from a 'formal review'?","back":"An informal review follows an author-reviewer cycle: the author sends the work product to a small group, collects comments informally, and decides freely whether/how to use them (often repeated over drafts). A formal review follows a prescribed way of working, used for milestone/final work products or high-risk situations (walkthroughs and inspections are the two main formal review types)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-4gcz7s","section":"4.4.2","level":"L2","front":"Compare alpha testing and beta testing as exploratory validation techniques.","back":"Alpha testing: done at the developer's site in a simulated environment, with a relatively small group and possible guidance - interactions can be observed directly (e.g., in a usability lab). Beta testing: done at the end users' sites in real production conditions, offered to a larger (often unknown) group, relying on participants to submit feedback after a period of real use."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-1ce304n","section":"4.4.2","level":"L2","front":"What is the essence of A/B testing, and in which methodology does it play a prominent role?","back":"The system is offered to different (usually randomly selected) groups of users in two variants that differ in design or functionality; the groups' reactions are measured and compared (works best with large groups for statistical analysis). A/B testing plays a prominent role in The Lean Startup design-thinking approach."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-5k02cb","section":"4.4.2","level":"L2","front":"What happens in 'sample development' as a validation technique, and why is it considered static?","back":"A set of requirements is given to developers, who try to produce intermediate work products (designs, code, test cases, manuals) based on them. It's static because the system itself isn't operative yet. Flaws (unclarities, omissions, inconsistencies) the developers hit while producing these artifacts indicate the quality of the requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch4-129rdl8","section":"4.4.2","level":"L1","front":"What validation effort can Requirements Engineers themselves perform that is similar to sample development?","back":"Converting a requirements specification from one representation into another (e.g., natural-language requirements into a model, or a model into a textual description) - this exercise is especially useful for detecting omissions; serious conversion problems indicate a need for additional validation."}],"5":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1ll3xdh","section":"5","level":"L1","front":"What does an RE process shape and define?","back":"It shapes the information flow and communication model between participants (customers, users, Requirements Engineers, developers, testers, etc.) and defines the work products to be used or produced - providing the framework for eliciting, documenting, validating, and managing requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1tgu3iz","section":"5","level":"L1","front":"Why must an RE process always be tailored rather than reused off-the-shelf?","back":"Because there is no one-size-fits-all RE process; a tailored RE process must be configured to fit the given development and system context."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-13qie1g","section":"5.1","level":"L1","front":"List the nine main factors that influence the configuration of an RE process.","back":"Overall process fit (fit with the system development process); development context; capability and availability of stakeholders; shared understanding; complexity and criticality of the system; constraints; available time and budget; volatility of requirements; experience of Requirements Engineers."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-mv2kt1","section":"5.1","level":"L2","front":"Give the syllabus's example of how an influencing factor constrains possible RE process configurations.","back":"If stakeholders are only available at the beginning of the project, no process can be chosen that relies on continuous stakeholder feedback."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1ipz098","section":"5.2","level":"L1","front":"Name the three decisive facets for configuring an RE process.","back":"Time facet (Linear vs. Iterative); Purpose facet (Prescriptive vs. Explorative); Target facet (Customer-specific vs. Market-oriented)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1d2u4uj","section":"5.2","level":"L1","front":"Time facet: Define a linear RE process and an iterative RE process.","back":"Linear: requirements are specified up front in a single phase of the process. Iterative: requirements are specified incrementally, starting with general goals and initial requirements, then adding/modifying requirements in every iteration."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-h6vkep","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"List the criteria favoring a LINEAR RE process.","back":"The development process is plan-driven/mostly linear; stakeholders know their requirements and can specify them up front; a comprehensive requirements specification is needed as a contractual basis for outsourcing; regulatory authorities require a comprehensive, formally released specification early on."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1y5vjrx","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"List the criteria favoring an ITERATIVE RE process.","back":"The development process is iterative/agile; many requirements are unknown up front and will emerge/evolve; stakeholders are available for short feedback loops (to mitigate the risk of building the wrong system); development duration allows for more than one or two iterations; the ability to change requirements easily is important."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-12kamqr","section":"5.2","level":"L1","front":"Purpose facet: Define a prescriptive RE process and an explorative RE process.","back":"Prescriptive: the requirements specification constitutes a contract - all requirements are binding and must be implemented. Explorative: only the goals are known a priori; the concrete requirements must be explored during the process."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-rep2sc","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"List the criteria favoring a PRESCRIPTIVE RE process.","back":"The customer requires a fixed development contract; functionality and scope take precedence over cost and deadlines; development may be tendered or outsourced."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1hh8p0r","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"List the criteria favoring an EXPLORATIVE RE process.","back":"Stakeholders initially only have a vague idea of their requirements; stakeholders are strongly involved and give continuous feedback; deadlines and cost take precedence over functionality and scope; it's not a priori clear which requirements will be implemented or in what order."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-4efcv1","section":"5.2","level":"L1","front":"Target facet: Define a customer-specific RE process and a market-oriented RE process.","back":"Customer-specific: the system is ordered by a customer and developed by a supplier. Market-oriented: the system is developed as a product/service for a market, targeting specific user segments."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-j889ff","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"List the criteria favoring a CUSTOMER-SPECIFIC RE process.","back":"The system will mainly be used by the ordering organization; important stakeholders are mainly from the customer's organization; individual persons can be identified for stakeholder roles; the customer wants a requirements specification that can serve as a contract."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-3f2xvz","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"List the criteria favoring a MARKET-ORIENTED RE process.","back":"The developing organization intends to sell the system in a market segment as a product/service; prospective users are not individually identifiable; requirements must be designed to match envisaged needs of targeted users; product owners, marketing people, digital designers, and system architects are the primary stakeholders."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1tjir5p","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"Give three 'hints and caveats' about combining RE process facets.","back":"Linear and prescriptive facets are frequently chosen together. Explorative processes are typically also iterative (and vice versa). The market-oriented facet does not combine well with the linear and prescriptive facets."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1bhgacq","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"Why do linear RE processes require intensive validation and a sophisticated change process?","back":"Linear processes imply long feedback loops, so to mitigate the risk of developing the wrong system, requirements must be validated intensively; and since requirements are mostly fixed up front, a sophisticated process for handling requirement changes must be in place for the linear process to work at all."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1wifyuv","section":"5.2","level":"L2","front":"In a market-oriented RE process, what is typically the only means of validating whether the product satisfies the target users' needs?","back":"Feedback from users (since prospective users are not individually identifiable up front, direct stakeholder involvement during specification is largely unavailable)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1t3lksf","section":"5.3","level":"L1","front":"Describe the 'Participatory RE Process' configuration: its facet combination, main application case, typical work products, and typical information flow.","back":"Facets: Iterative & Explorative & Customer-Specific. Application: supplier and customer collaborate closely; stakeholders strongly involved in both RE and development. Work products: product backlog with user stories/task descriptions, prototypes. Information flow: continuous interaction between stakeholders, product owners, Requirements Engineers, and developers; may include user feedback."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-jzpzks","section":"5.3","level":"L1","front":"Describe the 'Contractual RE Process' configuration: its facet combination, main application case, typical work products, and typical information flow.","back":"Facets: typically Linear (sometimes Iterative) & Prescriptive & Customer-Specific. Application: the requirements specification is the contractual basis for development by people not involved in specifying it, with little stakeholder interaction afterward. Work products: classic system requirements specification (natural-language requirements and models). Information flow: primarily from stakeholders to Requirements Engineers."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-13erdi6","section":"5.3","level":"L1","front":"Describe the 'Product-Oriented RE Process' configuration: its facet combination, main application case, typical work products, and typical information flow.","back":"Facets: Iterative & Explorative & Market-Oriented. Application: an organization specifies and develops software to sell/distribute as a product or service. Work products: product backlog, prototypes. Information flow: interaction between product owner, marketing, Requirements Engineers, digital designers, developers, and (maybe) fast feedback from customers/users."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-10j9kfm","section":"5.3","level":"L1","front":"Are the three typical RE process configurations (Participatory, Contractual, Product-Oriented) the only possible ones? Give an example of when none would fit.","back":"No - there may be system/development contexts where none of these fit, e.g. when regulatory constraints impose the use of a process conforming to a given standard such as ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch5-1wybzcy","section":"5.3","level":"L2","front":"List the recommended five-step procedure for configuring an RE process.","back":"1) Analyze the influencing factors (5.1). 2) Assess the facet criteria (5.2). 3) Configure the process (5.3). 4) Determine work products (Ch. 3). 5) Select appropriate practices."}],"6":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-q9om7j","section":"6.1","level":"L1","front":"Define 'requirements management' and name its core task.","back":"The process of managing existing requirements recorded in various work products - in particular, storing, changing, and tracing requirements. Its core task is to maintain requirements so that all roles in a project can work effectively and efficiently, regardless of the chosen development process/context."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1t31pnn","section":"6.2","level":"L2","front":"What is 'life cycle management', and what does a life cycle model need to define?","back":"The process of keeping track of all work products (and requirements) with respect to the status in their life cycle (created, evaluated, refined, reviewed, reworked, consolidated, agreed, etc.). A life cycle model must define each allowed life-cycle status and each allowed state transition, so the actual status of a work product is always clear (usually including its transition history)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-cid5yp","section":"6.2","level":"L2","front":"Why do individual requirements and the work products that contain them sometimes have life cycles that only 'partially overlap'? Give the handbook's example.","back":"Because a work product's overall state doesn't necessarily apply uniformly to every requirement inside it. Example: a definition study document can be 'under change' without every requirement in it needing to change; and 'implemented' makes no sense for the whole document - only some individual requirements in it get implemented (via code based on them)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1pg8lul","section":"6.2","level":"L2","front":"Why might different project roles (Requirements Engineer, project manager, product manager, change manager) be interested in different life-cycle states of the same requirement?","back":"Because each role cares about different points in the requirement's life - e.g., a Requirements Engineer's interest might end once a requirement is 'validated', while a project manager's interest might only start at 'documented'."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-rrmmqt","section":"6.2","level":"L1","front":"List the activities involved in life cycle management of work products/requirements.","back":"Defining life cycle models (allowed states, allowed transitions, triggering events); ensuring only explicitly allowed transitions occur; recording the actual states and transitions that occur; and reporting on these states and transitions."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1ac9kej","section":"6.3","level":"L1","front":"Give the two reasons for controlling versions of work products.","back":"1) Sometimes changes go wrong (defects found, benefits not realized) - versioning lets you roll back to or continue from a previous version. 2) You want to know the history/evolution of a work product from its origin to the present, to help with future change decisions or explain the current state."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-ja1wju","section":"6.3","level":"L1","front":"List the three measures required for version control to be in place.","back":"A version number to uniquely identify each version; a (clearly linked) description/history of what was changed; and a strict policy/concept for storing (and archiving) versions so old versions can be located and retrieved."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1b55ioe","section":"6.3","level":"L2","front":"What do the two parts of a typical version number ('version' and 'increment') represent?","back":"Version: starts at zero while under development; becomes version 1 on formal approval/release/launch; increased only for major, substantive updates thereafter. Increment: starts at one and is incremented with every externally visible change (content, text, or editorial); may have sub-increments for typo fixes."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-sa05m5","section":"6.3","level":"L2","front":"Does a change in a work product's life-cycle state (e.g., from 'validated' to 'implemented') normally require a new version number?","back":"No - a state change alone is usually not a reason to increment the version number, unless it is accompanied by an actual change in content or text. A requirement can move through several life-cycle states while keeping the same version number."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1vlmzs4","section":"6.4","level":"L1","front":"Define 'requirements configuration' and state its purpose.","back":"A consistent set of logically related work products containing requirements, containing at most one version of each work product, selected for a specific purpose - e.g., to review a set of work products, to support effort estimation, or to clarify which requirements were valid in a given situation."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-pww8op","section":"6.4","level":"L1","front":"List the five properties a correct configuration must have.","back":"Logical connection (logically connected set belonging together for a goal); Consistency (no internal conflicts, can be integrated); Uniqueness (configuration and its requirements are uniquely identified); Unchangeability (selected requirement versions in it never change); Basis for reset (allows fallback to a previous configuration)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-wp455g","section":"6.4","level":"L2","front":"What are the two dimensions of a configuration, according to the handbook?","back":"The product dimension (which requirements are included in the configuration - e.g., all requirements implemented in the French release) and the version dimension (exactly one specific version of each selected requirement is present; selecting even a single different version creates a new configuration)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1popusv","section":"6.4","level":"L1","front":"Define 'baseline' and give an example of when one is created.","back":"A baseline is a stable, validated, change-controlled configuration that marks a milestone or resting point in a project (used for release planning or delivery milestones). Example: the configuration at the end of the design phase before coding starts, or the configuration valid at the go-live of a release. (In agile projects, the sprint backlog serves as the baseline for the next iteration.)"},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1k89oxb","section":"6.4","level":"L2","front":"Why are baselines useful if something goes badly wrong in a project?","back":"Because they are stable, frozen reference points; if something goes terribly wrong, the team can perform a roll-back to the baseline's situation and restart from there."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-8pxu65","section":"6.5","level":"L1","front":"What is the objective of defining attributes for requirements?","back":"To enable team members and other stakeholders to get the information they need about requirements at any point during the project/product life cycle (metadata makes work products tangible and manageable, and answers questions like 'which requirements are planned for the next release?')."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1ptgxxk","section":"6.5","level":"L1","front":"List the attributes for requirements suggested by ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148.","back":"Identification (unique immutable ID); Stakeholder priority; Dependency (on other requirements); Risk (potential for problems if implemented); Source (origin of the requirement); Rationale (why it's needed); Difficulty (effort estimate); Type (functional/quality/constraint)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1bb0lwd","section":"6.5","level":"L1","front":"Define 'view' (of requirements) and state its purpose.","back":"A view is a combination of filter and sort settings applied to the requirements data, defined to deliver exactly the relevant information for a specific target group/audience - no more, no less."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-4huxea","section":"6.5","level":"L2","front":"Name and define the three types of views of requirements.","back":"Selective views - show a deliberate subset of requirements (e.g., only validated ones, or only high-priority ones), not all of them. Projective views - show a selection of attributes/data for the requirements (e.g., only ID, version, name), not all data. Aggregating views - show summaries, totals, or averages calculated from a set of requirements (e.g., number of requirements per department)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-lilije","section":"6.5","level":"L2","front":"Why are views usually combinations of selective, projective, and aggregating views?","back":"Because a report answering a real stakeholder question typically needs all three at once - e.g., a list with only the IDs, version numbers, names, and types (projective) of all requirements for the Sales department (selective) is a combined view."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1utfphb","section":"6.6","level":"L1","front":"Define 'traceability' and name the three kinds of trace relationships it covers.","back":"Traceability is the ability to trace a requirement back to its origin, forward to subsequent work products, and to other requirements it depends on. The three kinds of questions: backward traceability (origin/sources), forward traceability (what deliverables are based on it), and traceability between requirements (dependencies/refinements among requirements)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1icdz4s","section":"6.6","level":"L2","front":"Give five things that are hardly possible without proper traceability.","back":"Providing evidence that a requirement is satisfied; proving a requirement has been implemented and by what means; showing product compliance with laws/standards; finding missing work products (e.g., missing test cases); analyzing the effects of a requirements change."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1t5wby5","section":"6.6","level":"L1","front":"What is the difference between implicit and explicit traceability? Give an example of each.","back":"Implicit traceability is achieved by structuring/standardizing work products, e.g. via naming conventions (a code like Req-sal-012 implies the Sales department requested it) or document structure/templates. Explicit traceability relates work products to each other using their unique identifiers, e.g. via a traceability matrix, references/hyperlinks, or a trace graph."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1o1iaua","section":"6.6","level":"L1","front":"List common forms for documenting explicit traceability.","back":"Specific attributes such as 'Source'; references added in documents to predecessor documents/work products/requirements; a traceability matrix (spreadsheet or database table); Wiki-style hyperlinks in textual documentation; a trace graph visualizing the relationships."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-vnkvrk","section":"6.7","level":"L1","front":"Why do requirements changes always occur, and how are they typically formalized?","back":"Changes are not stand-alone events - they are triggered by changes in the system context, new stakeholder insights, competitor behavior, etc. (Principle 7: Evolution). They are typically formalized via a formal change request, or by adding a new item to the product backlog."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1b4ph06","section":"6.7","level":"L1","front":"How is the decision-making for handling a requirements change typically organized in a linear approach vs. an iterative approach?","back":"Linear approach: the decision is often taken by a Change Control Board (in projects) or a Change Advisory Board (in operation). Iterative approach: the product owner includes the change as a new item in the product backlog and prioritizes it accordingly."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1ambfkv","section":"6.8","level":"L1","front":"Why is prioritization of requirements necessary?","back":"Because resources (effort, time, money, attention) needed to implement requirements are limited, so not all requirements can be implemented at once - stakeholders must decide which come first, which come later, and which not at all."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1p1pxys","section":"6.8","level":"L1","front":"Define the 'priority' of a requirement.","back":"The level of importance assigned to a requirement according to certain (assessment) criteria, compared to other requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1bqk1w7","section":"6.8","level":"L1","front":"List the six steps for prioritizing requirements, in order.","back":"1) Define major goals and constraints for the prioritization. 2) Define desired assessment criteria. 3) Define the stakeholders to be involved. 4) Define the requirements to be prioritized. 5) Select the prioritization technique. 6) Perform prioritization."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-bzkduk","section":"6.8","level":"L1","front":"Give examples of commonly used assessment criteria for prioritization.","back":"Business value for stakeholders; urgency perceived by users; effort to implement; risk of usage; logical/technical dependencies; legally binding nature of the requirement; (inter-)subjective stakeholder preference."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1p2pbz0","section":"6.8","level":"L2","front":"What happens if different stakeholders apply the same prioritization technique and criteria but still disagree on the resulting priorities?","back":"This constitutes a requirements conflict, which should be resolved like any other conflict, using the conflict resolution techniques described in Section 4.3."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1velzxr","section":"6.8","level":"L1","front":"Name the two categories of prioritization techniques, and give two example ad-hoc techniques.","back":"Ad-hoc techniques and Analytical techniques. Example ad-hoc techniques: Top-10 ranking, and MoSCoW (Must have, Should have, Could have, Won't have this time)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-90qcv1","section":"6.8","level":"L2","front":"How can the Kano model (Section 4.2.1) be reused as an ad-hoc prioritization technique?","back":"Dissatisfiers are treated as must-haves, satisfiers as should-haves, and delighters as could-haves or won't-haves (this time)."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-19hqgk5","section":"6.8","level":"L2","front":"How do analytical prioritization techniques work, and what are their main advantage and two key risks?","back":"Experts assign weights to multiple assessment criteria (benefit, cost, risk, time, etc.); priorities are then calculated as weighted outcomes. Advantage: gives clear insight into what drives the priorities, aiding stakeholder acceptance. Risks: (1) the outcome is heavily influenced by the chosen weight factors, so stakeholders must agree on them beforehand to avoid manipulation; (2) the criteria assessed are usually estimates (often on a simple ordinal scale), so estimate quality limits prioritization quality."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch6-1pnxlgc","section":"6.8","level":"L2","front":"What is the risk of 'pseudo-accuracy' in analytical prioritization, and what principle should guide how much effort to invest in prioritization?","back":"Producing overly precise-looking scores (e.g., 22.76 vs. 23.12) can create a false sense of exactness when the underlying estimates don't support that precision - items that are really about equal might get falsely ranked. Guiding principle: Principle 1 (Value-orientation) - the effort spent prioritizing must be justified by the value gained from a more correct prioritization."}],"7":[{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-h9i7ic","section":"7.1","level":"L1","front":"Why do existing RE tools often focus only on certain aspects of RE rather than supporting all activities?","back":"Because the RE process is highly individual (tailored per Section 5) - so tools tend to specialize, and Requirements Engineers must decide which tasks/activities need support (and how) before selecting a tool."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-zozdt2","section":"7.1","level":"L1","front":"List the categories of RE tasks/activities that tooling can support.","back":"Management of requirements (attributes, prioritization, versions/configurations, tracking/tracing, change management); management of the RE process (measuring/reporting on process and product quality, managing RE workflow); documentation of knowledge about requirements (sharing requirements, creating shared understanding); modeling of requirements; collaboration in RE; testing/simulation of requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-a3p9oz","section":"7.1","level":"L1","front":"List the specific sub-activities that 'management of requirements' tools support.","back":"Defining and storing requirements attributes; prioritizing requirements; managing versions and configurations; tracking and tracing requirements; managing changes to requirements."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-57uh3q","section":"7.1","level":"L1","front":"List the specific sub-activities that 'management of the RE process' tools support.","back":"Measuring and reporting on the RE process; measuring and reporting on product quality; managing the RE workflow."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-ebxfip","section":"7.1","level":"L2","front":"Is it common for a single RE tool to cover only one of the tool categories (management of requirements, management of process, documentation, modeling, collaboration, testing/simulation)?","back":"No - tools often come in a mixture of these features. To ensure all RE tasks are covered appropriately, different tools might need to be combined."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-1tulrf6","section":"7.1","level":"L2","front":"When are generic tools like office software or issue-tracking tools acceptable substitutes for dedicated RE tools?","back":"They have limitations and should only be used when the RE process is in control and the requirements are aligned and quite stable."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-c57puf","section":"7.2","level":"L1","front":"What must be in place BEFORE an organization can successfully select an appropriate RE tool?","back":"The proper RE procedures and techniques must already have been introduced, and clear RE responsibilities and procedures must be established - an appropriate tool can only be sought once these are in place."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-1ks0lvg","section":"7.2","level":"L1","front":"What must be described before RE tool selection can be successful (just as for selecting any other tool)?","back":"The goal, the context, and the requirements (for the tool itself) must be described before selection can be successful."},{"id":"cpre-anki-ch7-106ea06","section":"7.2","level":"L1","front":"List the aspects relevant when introducing an RE tool into an organization.","back":"Take into account all life cycle costs beyond license costs; consider the necessary resources; use pilot projects to avoid risks; evaluate the tool according to defined criteria; instruct employees in how to use the tool."}]}};
